# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 6 on discourse.
# The superseded cycle-5 question is kept at
# scenarios/discourse.yaml.holder-subset-cycle5.bak (rubric alongside it).
#
# THE TRAJECTORY, EVERY CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW IT FELL ON
#   The window is BOTH conditions at once: baseline `dependents` <= 0.50 AND
#   delta >= +0.50. Sources are named per line; nothing here is recalled.
#
#   cycle 1  seven-step briefed session, 15 rows
#            MINI-BENCH  base 6/15 = 0.400   sense 14/15 = 0.933  delta +0.533
#                        side: INSIDE THE WINDOW, so it was expanded (minibench.1.md)
#            VALIDATION  base 11/15 = 0.733  delta +0.200
#                        side: BASELINE TOO HIGH (discourse.yaml.briefed-7step.bak)
#   cycle 2  two-step probe, 22 rows (call sites + declared associations)
#            base 12/22 = 0.5455  sense 14/22 = 0.6364  delta +0.091
#            side: BASELINE TOO HIGH (minibench.2.md, discourse.yaml.mixed-22row.bak)
#   cycle 3  two-step probe, 12 declared links
#            base 7/12 = 0.583   sense 11/12 = 0.917   delta +0.333
#            side: BASELINE TOO HIGH; recall caps at 1.00 so 0.583 capped the delta at
#            +0.417 before the pair ran (minibench.3.md, discourse.yaml.declared-link-12row.bak)
#   cycle 4  two-step probe, 14 one-touch call sites in 14 areas across 5 trees
#            MINI-BENCH  base 6/14 = 0.4286  sense 13/14 = 0.9286  delta +0.5000
#                        side: INSIDE THE WINDOW, on the bar exactly, so it was expanded
#            VALIDATION  base 8/14 = 0.5714  sense 0.9524  delta +0.3810  DO-NOT-PAY
#                        side: BASELINE TOO HIGH (minibench.4.md, validate.md,
#                        discourse.yaml.probe-2step-cycle4.bak / .session-7step-cycle4.bak)
#   cycle 5  two-step probe, 14 HELD REFERENCES, one per file, nine areas
#            base 4/14 = 0.2857  sense 6/14 = 0.4286  delta +0.1429
#            side: SENSE TOO LOW  (results/loop/discourse/minibench.md)
#
#   Cycles 1 to 4 all failed on the SAME side: the baseline over 0.50. Cycle 5 is the
#   FIRST landing on the far side, and it got there by moving the baseline to 0.2857 -
#   the lowest this repo has produced - while our own arm collapsed to 0.4286.
#
# WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM, AND HOW FAR IT INTENDS TO MOVE
#   From SENSE TOO LOW. The baseline half of the window is already won and this draft
#   does not touch the gold set that won it: all fourteen rows below are cycle 5's rows,
#   unchanged, every line re-opened and re-read against the clone before this draft was
#   written. The intended move is entirely on our arm and it is five rows: 6/14 -> at
#   least 11/14, which is +0.50 at an unchanged baseline of 4/14. If the baseline also
#   moves up, the move needed is larger; nothing here is aimed at moving it down further.
#
# WHY OUR ARM COLLAPSED, IN QUOTED OUTPUT
#   The cycle-5 sense run made four MCP calls, verbatim from its sense-io.jsonl
#   (results/loop/discourse/minibench.md quotes the same four):
#
#       sense_status {}
#       sense_search {"query": "resolve category from slug path, parent and child slug lookup", "limit": 20}
#       sense_search {"query": "query_category query_parent_category find_by_slug_path", "limit": 20, "language": "ruby"}
#       sense_search {"query": "category slug changed redirect permalink old slug", "limit": 20, "language": "ruby"}
#
#   All four are step-1 queries. `sense_blast` was never called and `sense_graph` was
#   never called; step 2 ran on 47 Bash greps. Our arm ran the baseline's route.
#
# THE ROWS ARE NOT UNREACHABLE - RUN, NOT ASSUMED
#   Nine rows were cited by neither arm in cycle 5 (inverse_frequency.py over
#   results/claude-opus-5/minibench/73e80a260d06f08b, "never cited by any arm : 9").
#   Because no blast was ever issued, that run could not say whether the payload carries
#   them. I issued it:
#
#       python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py "$CLONE" \
#         '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Category","file":"app/models/category.rb"}}]'
#
#   ALL FOURTEEN dependent files below appear in that shown payload - seven under
#   `direct_callers` (search_indexer.rb, reindex_search.rb, user_notifications.rb,
#   seed_data/topics.rb, uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb, poll_feed.rb, research/filter.rb)
#   and seven under `affected_via_composition` (web_hook.rb, tag_group.rb,
#   form_template.rb, reviewable.rb, category_tag_stat.rb, category_featured_topic.rb,
#   discourse_topic_voting/category_setting.rb). So "the tool cannot serve these rows" is
#   ruled out, and "the arm never asked the tool" is what is left standing.
#
# A FRICTION RECORDED, NOT ROUTED (it is not this phase's to fix)
#   With no `file` argument the same call comes back:
#
#       {"ambiguous": true, "hint": "Multiple symbols named \"Category\" exist - retry
#        this tool with the `file` parameter ...", "matches": 5}
#
#   Five symbols share the name (the Rails model, a frontend JS model, a dev seeder, a
#   page object, a migrations module). An arm that reaches step 2 without the model's
#   PATH in hand pays a disambiguation round trip before it sees anything.
#
# THE TWO CHANGES THIS DRAFT MAKES, AND WHY EACH
#   1. STEP 1 NOW ENDS ON A NAMED SYMBOL AND ITS PATH. Cycle 5's step 1 asked for an
#      end-to-end trace of how a typed path resolves to a record. The arm answered it in
#      prose and arrived at step 2 at 446s of a 480s wall (minibench.md) holding no
#      symbol - all three of its searches are sentences about slug resolution. This
#      draft's step 1 is a cheaper orientation on the class's own shape and lifecycle,
#      and it explicitly demands the class be named as it is written in the code, with
#      the file:line where it is defined. That is what makes step 2's first move
#      possible at all, and it is what makes the disambiguation above a non-event.
#   2. STEP 2 NO LONGER DISQUALIFIES THE WIDE SET; IT MAKES IT THE INPUT. Cycle 5's
#      step 2 opened "the question now is not who READS this class". A blast payload IS
#      a list of readers. Under that instruction the payload reads as answering the
#      wrong question, and the arm went to grep. THIS IS AN INFERENCE FROM THE WORDING
#      AND THE TOOL LOG, NOT A MEASURED CAUSE - the run is what tests it. This draft
#      keeps the holder/reader distinction, because that distinction is what put the
#      baseline at 0.2857 and it is load-bearing, but it reframes it as the METHOD: get
#      the widest list of everything that touches the class in front of you first,
#      because the answer is a subset of it, then work down that list deciding which
#      entries keep a reference and which merely read. The narrowing now sits in the
#      classification step, not in a sentence that rejects the tool's output shape.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   The answer is the set of places that are HOLDING a reference to one of these records
#   right now - a bare identifier kept in a column, a join row, a hand-written SQL
#   predicate, a queued job's arguments, a cache key, a stored setting - which a merge
#   that retires one record onto another would have to rewrite. That is a strict and
#   small subset of the places the class is mentioned: most mentions read a record and
#   hold nothing, and most holders do not name the class on the line where they hold it.
#   Nine of the fourteen rows below pin a line that does not contain the constant at all.
#
# WHY THAT IS A MECHANISM AND NOT AN INVENTORY
#   The step-2 ask names the FORMS a held reference takes - a column, a join row, a value
#   spliced into a query, a number in stored configuration or in a queued job's
#   arguments, a cache key. Those are ways of holding, not jobs code does. It never says
#   validation, worker, serializer, importer, task, migration or plugin - the functional
#   inventory that the neutrality gate passes clean and that hands the answer over.
#
# WHY THE MATCHER MAKES THE FILE SET THE WHOLE LEVER
#   `gold.py` credits a row CITED when the file path appears anywhere in the answer with
#   any line pin attached - the pin does not have to be the gold line. So re-pointing a
#   row's line at a harder expression inside a file the baseline already names buys
#   nothing. Rows are kept or dropped on which files an arm NAMES, and this draft keeps
#   the exact set that measured a baseline of 4/14.
#
# THE PER-FILE BASELINE RECORD THESE FOURTEEN ROWS CARRY (killer: per-unit, not aggregate)
#   Every valid baseline run on this repo on disk, scored per gold FILE, as compiled by
#   the cycle-5 draft and extended with cycle 5's own run:
#
#     0/7  d:migrations-optimizer
#     0/3  d:form-template   0/3  d:tag-group
#     1/3  d:web-hook            (0/2 before cycle 5, taken in cycle 5)
#     2/7  d:rss-polling
#     2/5  d:seed-topics     2/5  d:user-notifications
#     1/3  d:reindex-search-job  2/4  d:search-indexer
#     the four rows cycle 5's baseline took: d:category-featured-topic,
#     d:category-tag-stat, d:reviewable, d:web-hook
#
#   ARITHMETIC KILLER, RUN. Baseline 4/14 = 0.2857 leaves headroom 1.00 - 0.2857 =
#   0.7143, so +0.50 is not foreclosed before the pair runs. Reaching it needs sense at
#   >= 0.7857, i.e. 11 of 14. Sense reached 13/14 on cycle 4's fourteen rows and 14/15
#   on cycle 1's, so 11/14 is inside what this arm has already done on this repo - but
#   only when it called blast, which is exactly what is under test here.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py --file app/models/category.rb, RANKING ONLY)
#   Category              app/models/category.rb            app caller files 89,
#                         SCATTER 47 dirs, GREP-NOISE 109 app files,
#                         PRECISION 0.82   VERDICT "SCATTERED + grep-clean"  <- chosen
#   CategoryCustomField   app/models/category_custom_field.rb  app callers 9, SCATTER 8,
#                         grep-noise 3 app files, PRECISION 3.00
#   CategoryFeaturedTopic app/models/category_featured_topic.rb app callers 6, SCATTER 5,
#                         grep-noise 4 app files, PRECISION 1.50
#   0.82 sits in the tie zone (a deliberate control tie profiled 0.78, the +1.00 winner
#   0.078) and this is the sixth cycle on it. Recorded, not worked around: precision
#   ranks and never kills, and the two alternatives cannot yield twelve dependent files
#   across six areas from their shown blast sets (9 and 6 caller files), so Category is
#   the only anchor in this family that can carry the group. The law is iterate on the
#   question, keep the anchor - and cycle 5 measured the baseline at 0.2857 on it, which
#   is the best half-window this repo has produced.
#
# RING: ring_sweep.py --top 12 prints "0 candidate(s) above the bar" - all twelve anchors
#   report rows 0, files 0, vias 0, Category among them. The "what HOLDS X" kind in its
#   RETENTION sense is off the menu on this stack. (This draft asks who holds a
#   REFERENCE, which is a data question the composition edges answer, not a lifetime
#   question the empty ring would have to answer.)
#
# MEMORIZATION: memorization_probe.py Category --json -> samples [0.196, 0.0, 0.0],
#   "not recited". Every recited name is a member of Category itself (find_by_slug_path,
#   ensure_slug, downcase_email, ...). No gold row below is a member of Category.
#
# NEGATIVE SPACE, RUN NOT ASSUMED. Over the files in the shown sense_blast payload, the
#   ones carrying zero occurrences of the bare constant all sit in one directory
#   (app/models). There is no scattered token-dark file set on this anchor to curate for
#   and this draft does not pretend there is: five of its fourteen rows pin a line that
#   names the constant. Recorded, deciding nothing - token-darkness is not the mechanism.
#
# THE NAMED RISK OF THIS DRAFT
#   Telling the arm to start from the widest list is symmetric: the baseline reads the
#   same sentence, and its cycle-5 route was already 46 calls and 40 searches of exactly
#   that shape. If the run comes back with the baseline back over 0.50, the reading is
#   that the "widest list first" framing licensed a dump and the narrowing has to move
#   back into a disqualifying sentence - in which case the lever is the scorer's
#   recall-only credit, not the wording, because a recall-only metric cannot charge an
#   arm for listing readers it was told not to list.
# ============================================================================
name: Discourse work session - audit the category contract before a rework
repo: discourse
contract_symbol: Category
contract_file: app/models/category.rb

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the class that represents one of this forum's
  top-level containers - the thing topics are filed under, that carries its own
  permissions, settings and hierarchy, and that half the product reaches for whenever it
  needs to know where something belongs or who is allowed to see it. The rework makes two
  of these records mergeable: one of them is retired, and everything still pointing at the
  retired one has to be made to point at the survivor. Before you touch it you have to
  know two things: what the record itself is made of and what already happens to the rest
  of the system when one is created, renamed or deleted, and everywhere in the codebase
  that is holding on to one of them in a form that merge would have to rewrite.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate
  could jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not
  count, a set of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of
  them does not count, and a holder you miss is a dangling pointer shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the category contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and keep it tight - the rest of the ticket is the expensive part and
      you will want your budget for it. Find the class that represents this container and
      state, in the first line of your answer, its exact name as it is written in the
      code together with the file:line where it is defined, so the rest of the work has a
      single unambiguous subject; if more than one thing in this codebase carries that
      name, say which one is the one under rework and where the others live. Then map the
      record itself as a maintainer would have to understand it before merging two of
      them: what it stores in its own right, what other records it declares a link to and
      which of those links are owned outright rather than shared, what it refuses to
      accept, and - this is the part that matters most for a merge - everything the
      system already does automatically when one of these is saved, renamed or destroyed,
      because a merge has to run the retired record's version of all of it. Then show
      where one of these records is created, edited and deleted from the outside. For
      each thing you name give the file:line and the routine or declaration it lives in,
      and one phrase on what it does. Close with a few sentences on what a merge would
      have to reconcile between the two records, and what that automatic behaviour means
      for the one being retired. A place named without the routine or declaration it
      lives in does not count, and a name given without the file:line where it is defined
      does not count.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "8"
        required: true
        description: The subject named and located, its shape and automatic lifecycle behaviour mapped, each item located
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the category contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the
      first part already cost you. The merge retires one of these records onto another, so
      what you owe is every place that is HOLDING a reference to one of them right now, in
      a form the merge would have to find and rewrite. Work it in that order: first get
      the widest list you can of everywhere in this codebase that touches this class in
      any way at all, because the answer is a subset of that list and you cannot pick the
      subset without seeing it; then go down the list and decide, one entry at a time,
      which ones actually keep something. Most of them do not - they look a record up, use
      it and keep nothing, and a read is not a holder. The ones that do keep something
      mostly do not name the class on the line where they hold it. What a holder keeps is
      a bare identifier: a column on its own table; a row in a join table that some other
      model populates on its behalf; a value spliced into a hand-written query string; a
      number parked in stored configuration, or in the arguments of a job that was queued
      yesterday and runs tomorrow; a key in a cache. Those lines read like ordinary data
      and say nothing about what they point at, so deciding whether an entry is a holder
      means opening it and working out what it does with what it kept - the wide list
      tells you where to look, it does not tell you the answer. For each holder you keep,
      give the file:line where the reference is actually held - not where the class is
      mentioned nearby - the routine or declaration it sits in, and one phrase on what
      would be left dangling, stale or pointed at the wrong record if the merge skipped
      it. Organise the answer by area, one section per area, and treat an area as finished
      only when every holder inside it is listed separately with its own location; if you
      cannot finish an area, say which one and say what you left in it, because an area
      you quietly summarised reads as an area with nothing in it. A file:line with nothing
      named around it does not count, a holder you cannot state the consequence for does
      not count, and a holder you miss is a dangling pointer shipped.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every holder located at file:line with its own dangling-reference consequence
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre, its declared shape, its automatic lifecycle and
  # ---- its outside write path. Both arms reach these and they do NOT score. Re-cut for
  # ---- cycle 6's step 1 (cycle 5's anchors were the slug-resolution path, which this
  # ---- step no longer asks for). Every line below was opened and read against the clone.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [app/models/category.rb]
    relation: "app/models/category.rb:3 `class Category < ActiveRecord::Base` - the contract under rework; :19-:74 are the declared links a merge has to reconcile (`belongs_to :topic` at :19, `has_many :topics` at :32, the owned-outright ones carrying `dependent: :destroy` at :36-:52, the through-links at :47-:50 and `has_and_belongs_to_many :web_hooks` at :74), :79-:119 the validations that would refuse a merged record, and :121-:143 the automatic lifecycle a merge must run for the retired record (`before_save :apply_permissions` at :121, `after_create :create_category_definition` at :126, `after_update :create_category_permalink, if: :saved_change_to_slug?` at :129, `after_destroy :trash_category_definition` at :132 and `after_destroy :clear_related_site_settings` at :133), with a further anonymous `after_save do` block at :145"
  - id: c:categories-controller
    group: contract
    match: [app/controllers/categories_controller.rb]
    relation: "app/controllers/categories_controller.rb:157 `def create`, :223 `def update` and :332 `def destroy` - the outside write path; :334 `@category.destroy` is the call that fires the whole after_destroy chain above, and :335 `Discourse.cache.delete(Categories::TypeRegistry::COUNTS_CACHE_KEY)` deletes a counts cache key by hand immediately after it"
  - id: c:site-cache
    group: contract
    match: [app/models/site.rb]
    relation: "app/models/site.rb:64 `def self.all_categories_cache` - the whole set is cached for thirty minutes at :73 `.fetch(categories_cache_key, expires_in: 30.minutes)`, and the comment at :68-:69 states the invariant a merge has to honour: any new eager-loaded association needs a corresponding ActiveRecord callback to clear this cache; :60 `def self.clear_cache` is the other half and :56 `def self.categories_cache_key` is the key it deletes"
  - id: c:category-guardian
    group: contract
    match: [guardian/category_guardian.rb]
    relation: "lib/guardian/category_guardian.rb:61 `def allowed_category_ids`, whose body at :64 is `unrestricted = Category.where(read_restricted: false).pluck(:id)` concatenated at :65 with :56 `def secure_category_ids` - permission is answered as a set of bare ids, so who may see the survivor after a merge is decided here and not on the record"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: fourteen REFERENCE HOLDERS, one per file, nine distinct areas.
  # ---- UNCHANGED from cycle 5, which measured a baseline of 4/14 = 0.2857 on them.
  # ---- Every file below appears in the shown sense_blast payload for Category
  # ---- (seven direct_callers, seven affected_via_composition) - checked by running
  # ---- mcp_probe before this draft was written, not assumed. Every line below was
  # ---- re-opened and re-read against the clone. Nine pin a line without the constant.

  # -- area: app/models - references held in the holder's own data
  - id: d:web-hook
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/web_hook.rb]
    relation: "app/models/web_hook.rb:111 `category_id: topic.category_id,` inside `WebHook.enqueue_topic_hooks` (def at :99) - the bare id is copied into the arguments of an outbound hook job that runs later and is delivered to an external system, so nothing re-reads the record; the habtm declaration at :6 is the second holder, a join row per subscribed container. A merge that missed this leaves queued and delivered payloads naming a container that no longer exists, and leaves subscription rows pointed at the retired id"
  - id: d:tag-group
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/tag_group.rb]
    relation: "app/models/tag_group.rb:125 `id IN (SELECT tag_group_id FROM category_tag_groups WHERE category_id IN (?))` inside `TagGroup.visible` (def at :113) - the id is spliced into a hand-written SQL subselect against the join table declared at :19-:21, so the reference is held in raw SQL text and in join rows the model never names the class to reach. A merge that missed this leaves tag groups visible only under the retired id and invisible under the survivor"
  - id: d:form-template
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/form_template.rb]
    relation: "app/models/form_template.rb:18 `has_many :categories, through: :category_form_templates` - the reference is held entirely as rows in the join table declared at :17; the line that holds it names neither the class nor an id column. A merge that missed this leaves a form template attached to the retired container and unattached to the survivor"
  - id: d:reviewable
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/reviewable.rb]
    relation: "app/models/reviewable.rb:463 `\"reviewables.category_id IS NULL OR reviewables.category_id IN (?)\",` inside `Reviewable.viewable_by` (def at :424) - the stored id on the reviewable's own row is filtered on through a hand-written SQL predicate. A merge that missed this leaves review queue items keyed to the retired container, so they disappear from every moderator's view scoped to the survivor"
  - id: d:category-tag-stat
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/category_tag_stat.rb]
    relation: "app/models/category_tag_stat.rb:20 `AND category_id = :category_id` inside the heredoc SQL of `CategoryTagStat.topic_moved` (def at :7) - a denormalised per-container tag counter whose rows are found and decremented by raw id in hand-written SQL. A merge that missed this leaves two disjoint counter sets, one under each id, and every tag count under the survivor understated"
  - id: d:category-featured-topic
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/category_featured_topic.rb]
    relation: "app/models/category_featured_topic.rb:20 `next_category_id = batched ? Discourse.redis.get(NEXT_CATEGORY_ID_KEY).to_i : 0` inside `CategoryFeaturedTopic.feature_topics` (def at :11) - a bare id parked in a cache under the key declared at :7, held across job runs as the cursor the batched featuring resumes from. A merge that missed this leaves the cursor parked on a retired id, so the batch loop either stalls or skips the survivor"

  # -- area: app/services
  - id: d:search-indexer
    group: dependents
    match: [app/services/search_indexer.rb]
    relation: "app/services/search_indexer.rb:226 `update_index(table: \"category\", id: category_id, a_weight: name)` inside `SearchIndexer.update_categories_index` (def at :225) - the search-data row is written keyed on the bare id and holds a copy of the name; the reindex queue at :236 splices the same id into heredoc SQL. A merge that missed this leaves search rows under the retired id, so the retired container keeps matching searches and the survivor's own row is stale"

  # -- area: app/jobs/scheduled
  - id: d:reindex-search-job
    group: dependents
    match: [scheduled/reindex_search.rb]
    relation: "app/jobs/scheduled/reindex_search.rb:113 `.joins(\"LEFT JOIN category_search_data ON category_id = categories.id\")` inside `Jobs::ReindexSearch#load_problem_category_ids` (def at :111) - the staleness sweep joins the search table on the raw id in a hand-written join string. A merge that missed this leaves orphaned search rows that this sweep can never see, because they no longer join to any live record"

  # -- area: app/mailers
  - id: d:user-notifications
    group: dependents
    match: [app/mailers/user_notifications.rb]
    relation: "app/mailers/user_notifications.rb:633 `\"#{Category.where(id: category.parent_category_id).pick(:name)}/#{show_category_in_subject}\"` inside `UserNotifications#send_notification_email` (def at :599), guarded at :631 - the parent id held on the child's own row is resolved to build the email subject prefix. A merge that missed this sends notification subjects prefixed with the name of a retired parent, or with a blank where the prefix should be"

  # -- area: lib/seed_data
  - id: d:seed-topics
    group: dependents
    match: [seed_data/topics.rb]
    relation: "lib/seed_data/topics.rb:63 `general_category = Category.find_by(id: SiteSetting.general_category_id)` inside `SeedData::Topics#topics` (def at :57), with two more ids read the same way at :64 and :65 - three bare ids parked in stored configuration and dereferenced here. A merge that missed this leaves the configured pointers on the retired id, so seeded topics land nowhere or the seeder silently skips them"

  # -- area: migrations/ (the ONE row outside app|lib|plugins; it is the single strongest
  # -- record on this repo at 0/7 baseline, kept for that reason and not as a path moat)
  - id: d:migrations-optimizer
    group: dependents
    match: [uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb]
    relation: "migrations/lib/uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb:43 `@category_id = Category.last.id` inside `Migrations::Uploader::Tasks::Optimizer#initialize_discourse_resources` (def at :40) - one arbitrary id is captured up front into an instance variable and then carried as plain data into every synthetic upload built at :172 `category: @category_id,`. A merge that missed this leaves the optimiser attaching uploads to a retired container for the rest of the run"

  # -- area: plugins/
  - id: d:rss-polling
    group: dependents
    match: [discourse_rss_polling/poll_feed.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-rss-polling/app/jobs/jobs/discourse_rss_polling/poll_feed.rb:14 `@discourse_category_id = args[:discourse_category_id]` inside `Jobs::DiscourseRssPolling::PollFeed#execute` (def at :8) - the id arrives as a queued job argument out of stored feed configuration, is checked for existence at :15 and is stamped onto every created topic at :64 `category_id: discourse_category_id,`. A merge that missed this leaves already-queued polls filing imported items into the retired container, or aborting on a container that no longer exists"
  - id: d:topic-voting-setting
    group: dependents
    match: [discourse_topic_voting/category_setting.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-topic-voting/app/models/discourse_topic_voting/category_setting.rb:18 `WHERE topics.category_id = :category_id` inside the heredoc SQL of `DiscourseTopicVoting::CategorySetting#unarchive_votes` (def at :13, run as a before_create at :9) - the id held on this plugin table's own row (association at :7, table named at :5) is spliced into hand-written SQL that reaches the votes through topics, and again at :31 in `#archive_votes`. A merge that missed this leaves the retired container's votes archived or unarchived on their own, out of step with the survivor's setting"
  - id: d:ai-research-filter
    group: dependents
    match: [research/filter.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-ai/lib/utils/research/filter.rb:141 `found_category_ids << category.id if category` inside the block passed to `register_filter(/\\A(?:categories?|category):(.*)\\z/i)` at :134 - the filter sits in no def; it is a block registered in the class body. Names typed by a user are resolved once to bare ids at :140, accumulated into a list, and it is that id list, not the names, that the query predicate is built from at :145. A merge that missed this leaves a stored research filter resolving to an id that matches nothing"
